Say whether a membership request ever reaches this daemon.

A client asking libinfo to translate a uid never gets an answer: trustd blocks in mbr_uid_to_uuid
before it can create its own XPC listener, so a running, checked-in trustd answers every request
with silence. A ten-line probe reproduces the same hang outside trustd, as root.

This daemon is the other end of that call, and it says nothing, because everything it logs goes
through os_log and the container has no log sink. These go to stderr, which launchd captures in
StandardErrorPath.

Traces at each of the three points, because "no reply" has three different causes and they are
indistinguishable from outside: the listener never fired, the connection never delivered the
dictionary, or a reply was sent that never arrived.

WHAT IT ANSWERED, and it moved the bug:

    CIDER_ODD main entered
    CIDER_ODD creating the mach service listener
    CIDER_ODD listener created = 0x752e04d78190
    CIDER_ODD calling xpc_connection_resume on the listener
    CIDER_XPC check-in for service com.apple.system.opendirectoryd.membership = 0 (claimed)
    CIDER_ODD listener resumed, entering dispatch_main

So the daemon is entirely up: listener created, service claimed, parked in dispatch_main. And while
a client sits blocked in xpc_pipe_routine, the listener event handler NEVER FIRES. Not a slow
daemon, not a crashed one, not a failed check-in.

It is also not the listener throwing the message away: libxpc drops any non-checkin message a
listener receives, that path is instrumented too, and it does not fire either. The message is lost
before it gets that far.

The environment dump is here for a reason worth keeping. A launchd plist that gains a SECOND
EnvironmentVariables key becomes a duplicate-key dict, the parser silently takes one, and the added
variables never arrive -- which reads exactly like the traces not running. Printing the environment
as it actually arrived settles that in one run instead of by argument.
